# What the packaged builds (AppImage, Windows installer, Snap) need on top of
# npyquick's own dependencies, plus the version limits chosen on purpose.
#
# This is the input. packaging/constraints.txt is generated from it and from
# pyproject.toml, and pins every package to an exact version; the three builds
# install with that file as a pip constraint, so they all ship the same
# versions. A pip install of npyquick is not affected: pyproject.toml only
# sets lower bounds.
#
# To move to newer versions, regenerate the file and review the diff:
#
#   uv pip compile pyproject.toml packaging/requirements.in \
#       --universal --python-version 3.12 --upgrade \
#       -o packaging/constraints.txt
#
# Do it at the start of a release cycle rather than just before a release, so
# the new versions get some use first. The packaging CI then builds all three
# and runs `npyquick --self-test` in each.

pyinstaller

# Qt 6.12 made a sandboxed Wayland application receive dragged files through
# the desktop portal's file transfer instead of as plain paths. Inside the
# strict snap that fails, and Qt's own fallback does not recover: dragging a
# .npy onto the window is refused and the terminal shows
#
#   qt.qpa.wayland: Failed retrieving files, falling back to uris
#
# Seen in the first 0.1.5 snap, built with 6.12.0. The 0.1.4 snap, built with
# an earlier Qt, was fine, as were the 0.1.5 snap on X11 and the unsandboxed
# AppImage on Wayland. Only the snap needs this limit, but all three builds
# share one set of versions. To re-test: remove the limit, regenerate, build
# the snap, and drop a file on the window in a Wayland session.
PySide6-Essentials<6.12
